===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|greve|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|grǣfe}}, {{term|græfe|grǣfa|bush, bramble, grove, thicket, copse, brush-wood (for burning), fuel|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*grainiz||twig}}, of unknown origin. Cognate with {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|grein||branch, bough|lang=non}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|grāf}}, {{m|ang|grāfa||grove}}. See {{l|en|grove}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A [[bush]]; a [[tree]]; a [[grove]].
#: {{rfquotek|Spenser}}
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A [[bough]]; a [[branch]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|greve|lang=enm}}, {{term|greyve|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|grǣfa}}, {{m|ang|grēfa||pit, cave, hole, grave, trench}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*grōbō||pit, ditch}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*gʰrebʰ-||to dig, scratch, scrape}}. Cognate with {{etyl|frr|-}} {{term|groop||pit, sewer, gutter|lang=frr}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|groef||pit, hole, gutter|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Grube||pit, hole|lang=de}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|gröf||pit, grave|lang=is}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A [[ditch]] or [[trench]].

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|greve|lang=enm}}, {{term|grayve|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|greve||shin|lang=fro}}, of unknown origin.

